Hello all,

I will soon be starting a USENET server for our K12 network.  We are a large 
district with some 60,000+ students at some 130 sites.  As the person who will 
be responsible for managing which groups we subscribe to I was curious about 
the stance of others in the same boat.

As a K12 school district, do you not subscribe to alt.sex.* or 
alt.binaries.pictures.* and hence deny access to all network users or only 
restrict access to students or, do you subscribe to these hierarchies?  Until 
I started poking around the net and found this group as well as a WWW page 
going into the ALA stance on this and other related issues, I had assumed that 
since we were a K12 institute that the question of "is it right to not 
subscribe to those hierarchies" wouldn't come up.  The answer would be, "are 
you kidding!? Of course not."

Understand too that we are a large network and our eventual goal is to provide 
Internet access to all 60,000 students including those in the elementary 
schools.  Is it really proper to allow them access to 
alt.sex.stories.pedophilia?  Or some of the other groups?  Should we allow 
parents a say in what groups we subscribe to?  Should we subscribe to 
everything but allow access only if a parent allows it?

I would be very interested to hear other people comments about this, 
particulary from those who administer K12 districts.

Richard


