First, I recommend checking the minor-access to sexually explicit
material laws in your state.  Since discussion groups serve a
significant educational value, they may be exempt, and you can save
yourself the trouble of becoming a censor.

CMU currently provides USENET feeds to a local highschool, and has
done so for a while (since before CMU started broad unreasonable
censorship of the binaries groups).  What computing services did was
come up with a list of "probably troublesome", and "possibly
troublesome" newsgroups.  These lists were provided to the high
school, and the high school made the decision of what groups to forbid
access to by their students.  Access control lists were used to remove
rights to those bboards for that school's students only.

I'm not fond of this solution, because it restricts a lot of perfectly
legal discussion, but is is better than an outright removal of the groups.

I would strongly recommend against banning "alt.sex.*".  alt.sex.safe
is a good example of a group which should be made available to K12
students.

