Finally, someone who understands that this is an issue, rather than
saying that universities are exempt....

: For example, a number of unscrupulous people using rec.arts.startrek.*
: posted the entire script from Paramount's "Star Trek: Generations" movie, a
: month before the picture opened.  This is clearly a serious copyright
: violation and the posting of such a script must have cost Paramount revenues
: in lost ticket sales.  In years past, others have done the same with other
: Star Trek movies in the rec.arts.startrek.* newsgroups.

True.  This however, is a minority of the posts in those newsgroups, and
it's my understanding from reading other media sources that Paramount
has threatened to sue *individuals* who engage in sourcing the material
onto the newsgroups again.  In the *.binaries.pictures.* newsgroups, 
erotic or no, the majority of the information is geared towards circumventing 
the law.  It was pointed out to me tonight that at leat two of the newsgroups 
have the consent of the artists (*.boris and *.nagel.)  The rest, however, 
don't.  This opens up a whole can of worms to the lawyers.

: But on any given newsgroup, there are people who post copyrighted articles
: with the disclaimer "Reposted without permission" (as if this somehow makes
: it OK to do) from magazines and newspapers.

Yes, this is true, and it's a problem, but it is NOT the majority of the
posts to the newsgroup, and may be similar to citing a passage in a
paper without obtaining the author's permission (it's unclear - I'm not
a legal specialist.)

Let's take *.binaries.pictures.erotica as an example.  If a majority of the 
pictures were uncopywritten amature production, and were made available
to prove a point or support an idea, or even to titilate the audience, 
I don't think there would be an issue (at least with CMU).  A quick look 
through the group, however, shows that this is not the case. (Example: 
Tonya Harding Wedding Night pictures.  What's the reason to post these other 
than titilation, or "appealing solely to the purient interest?")

: Then there are the stolen articles that are reposted without permission from
: the Clarinet newsgroups.  These copyright violations occur far more often than
: any other copyright infringment on the net.

Clarinet's dave.barry and mike.royko groups were pulled in response to
threatened lawsuits over the reproduction of works to other groups by
the syndicators of the columnists.  (This is the way it wqs presented
here.  If that's not the case, I've been locally misinformed.)
I think this is a valid precedent.

: So one has to ask: if the occasional copyright violation is reason enough to
: remove the alt.binaries.pictures.* groups (as if WAV files in alt.binaries.
: sounds.misc of Bart Simpson aren't copyright violations) then shouldn't we
: be removing most, if not all, of the so-called mainstream USENET newsgroups?

No, occasional copywrite violations aren't a valid reason to pull a
group, just like the occsional file found on a BBS or an archive site
without the knowledge of the sysop/admin is not grounds for a lawsuite. 
If you scan a newpaper closely enough, you can probably find a
copywrite violation in almost every issue.  When a group is geared
towards behavior that consistently violates the law, however, the story
changes. (In my opinion, if the alt.binaries.sounds.misc files are
violating copywrite laws, they should go too.)  To site a parallel
example, look at the number of sysops who have been procecuted for
running pirate BBSes.  It's rare to see copywritten software on
University FTP sites as a result.

: Whether it's a picture of "Miss May" or the script from Paramount's latest
: Star Trek movie, a copyright violation is a copyright violation.

True, but see above.  Perhaps it is a matter of degree, the law isn't
clear on this point, and I think that any provider, university or not,
is stupid if they don't consider the possibilities.  What would the
reaction be if CMU, since they ARE one of the most visible internet
providers, were to be sued over making copywritten images available?

It's much easier to sue a hundred universities to shut down the feed
than it is to figure out who made the picture available and prosecute
the individual.  Probably more profitable, too.  (Another thing I was
told tonight: netcom is being sued by Playboy to prevent access to the
*.binaries.erotica newsgroups over copywrite violations.  Can anyone
confirm or deny?  This is secondhand information and I'm not sure
wheather or not to believe it, even though it would prove my point.)

Removing access to the group doesn't restrict the availability of the
image - you just don't get it for "free."  You have to buy the
magazine/movie/artwork.  This goes back to another article I posted,
stating that the idea that all net access should not only be free, but
legally exempt, is stupid.

: I get the feeling that this is more an excuse to remove "erotic" material
: than to somehow cleanse the net from evil copyright violations.

I'm not contesting that this may be the agenda of some of the people.
What I keep saying: There ARE reasonable positions that may cause the
removal of SOME newsgroups that violate the law.  No one has been able
to address the specific questions I've asked.  Who's to say that a court
wouldn't say, based on the precedents I've sited, that the groups
represent a violation, and require restitution to the original
artists/copywriters of the material?

: In any case, I believe that this is yet another case where the law has not
: kept up with technology.  Clearly, those posting the stolen materials are
: liable for the infringment.  The question is whether all the sites along
: the way that had the materials transmitted to them are liable as well.

This is definately an unclear area of the law wrt Usenet.  BBSes ARE
responsible for providing this information through echomail (or whatever
they're calling it this year.)  Several cases have been decided in the
past several years holding system operators and providers responsible for the
actions of their users if it became known to the admins that the users' actions
violated the law.  They're also liable, under state laws, for
correspondance that violates local decency laws.  While CMU's actions
may not have violated PA laws, it is possible that some of the groups
would violate local laws in other states (Georgia comes to mind wrt some
of the stuff on alt.sex.stories or even alt.sex.  Yes, I've read them
off and on - I'm a red blooded american male, dammit. :-))  While
alt.sex may not open up CMU to local decency laws, who's to say that it
wouldn't open up Georgia Tech.  Should GT not pull the group to head off
a lawsuit?

It is concivable that the rulings can be extended to cover news sites that 
originate postings of erotic or non-erotic copywritten material, or even
those who carry the groups in question, once someone has notified the
admins of the potential problem. This has yet to be challenged in court, 
to the best of my knowledge.  Perhaps someone who knows more about the 
outcome of these cases than what's been available in the press could clarify....
The ACLU only addressed the local decency law issue, not the rest of the
issues I've cited.  I'd be interested to hear their opinion.

