I'm more than disappointed with the kind of approach I see fellow 'Net
users take towards issues like CMU's attempt to restrict access to sexually
oriented images on the Internet.  We wrap a santimonious mantle of
academic and personal freedoms about our defense of material that is inherently
exploitational, profoundly anti-human - and decidedly juvenile in it's appeal.  
While we make a lot of noise about our theoretical rights, we erode our case 
before the public as a whole in our choice of what we are defending.  Too often
we sound very much like we are saying, "Because I WANT this stuff, I have
a RIGHT to have someone GIVE ME ACCESS TO IT."  And that's all the reason
we feel we need.

I think that the ease of access many of us have to network resources
- and the fact that we may not pay directly for them -
cloaks the distinction between _permitting access_ to something, and 
_facilitating access_ to that thing.  For example, no one debates our right
to buy sexually oriented printed material if we choose to do so.  That's
a different matter than claiming that a university library is obligated in
the name of academic freedom to maintain subscriptions to as many pornographic
periodicals as they can.  In the same way, I believe it's possible to
hold that while there ought to be no censorship of what passes over the 
Internet, that doesn't mean that institutions are obligated to devote disk 
space or bandwidth to facilitate the distribution of pornographic binaries.  If 
you want to buy a high speed modem you can download such stuff to your hearts 
content from BBSs all over the country - and pay the cost yourself.  Want
to set up your own NNTP server?  Are you going to buy the workstation and
purchase Internet services from a commercial provider to do it, or were
you just going to cadge some space on a machine that uses the University
backbone?  Hmmmm.

A university doesn't have to be close-minded or "puritanical" to hold that
they have no duty to _actively support and provide resources for_ these
uses.  

Back to my original kvetch: the University's concern for it's own image
in permitting or forbidding the material in question is one thing.  What
escapes a lot of folks seems to be how much _their own_ image suffers
in demanding these newsgroups. Wish we didn't look so much like a 
bunch of junior high boys in this discussion . . . 
