I will apologize for the "junior high boys" crack and agree that this
isn't the issue, and I really should have left that kind of characterization
out of this.

I have difficulty - attribute it to whatever you will - equating binary
images that are unapologetically intended for prurient entertainment with
speech, ideas, or words.  This is especially true in that the images are
reproduced in violation of copyright laws.

In these kinds of debates, the actual "information" is often held to be a 
"black box", whose content is not relevant.  Only freedom of access matters.
What if the material violates not only the aforementioned copyright laws
but also community pornography laws?  More to the point, what if it is
nothing less than an active collaboration in the exploitation and abuse
of women and children?  What if it encourages the myth that the generation
of pornographic images is a benign industry involving only consenting
adults?  In a deeper sense, the production of these things involve crimes
not only from an ethical standpoint but in many cases from a legal standpoint.

Censorship is one thing.  Censure is another.  I guess we'll find out if
the 'Net is subjected to the former, but by no means ought we to feel like
we should be immune to the latter.  
