First off, I apologize for somewhat personalizing this argument - it was
out of line and uncalled for.  No excuses.

Now, wheather or not there were other legitimate need for resources is
irrelevant.  As the "owner" of the resources, CMU gets to determine how
they're allocated.  Period.  The students, as a part of the university,
with a clearly defined voice in the administrative procedure, have a
part in that decision making process.  The University adminstration did
something, the students (consumers) did something else, and the
University responded.  That's the American way, isn't it?

: Now tell me, Bill, which part(s) of "the CMU Admin, will not allow the 
: students to set up their own NNTP Server ... even if they pay for the 
: equipment themselves" are *you* unable to comprehend?

Because CMU still is paying for the network by allocating a budget to
the PSC (or Prepnet, but I'm not exactly sure how the money flows,)
they get to decide how people tie into their feed.  

CMU is not, and CANNOT prevent students from purchasing equipment
(computers, routers/CSU/DSU units, hard drives dedicated phone lines,
etc), getting an internet address through a service provider and PAYING 
for a net feed.  They also cannot prevent CMU students from accessing Telerama 
or Pitt, to get the information.  All they've effectively said is that
"We won't sell this access to you."

If CMU were doing what you're claiming they are by preventing students
to use their own equipment to access the information, Pitt, Telerama 
and other service providers would not be receiving the "banned groups,"  
and CMU would be attempting to pull the ability of the students to
utilize their own equipment to access outside providers.  They haven't
done a single thing along these lines.  There would be some sort of 
disciplinary action for students using AT&T to call out, or caught reading the 
groups.  This is not the case.

If you want to tie into CMU, through THEIR feed, they get to specify
what the rules are.  Clear enough?  If CMU came out and said something
like, "If you're a student here, you're not allowed to read x, y and z,"
I would have a major problem with it.  All they've said is, "Hey, it's
our equipment, we're the administration, and we're gonna decide how we
use it.  We've decided we don't want to use it that way."  Just because 
a single student can't afford the $5k a year a Prepnet feed costs doesn't 
obligate CMU to provide that service.  Period.  Note that they also have
not pulled gopher, Mosiac, or Netscape service to the best of my
knowledge.  All they've done is restrict what their disk space and some
of their available routing bandwidth is used for.  (You could make an 
argument that this renders their policy ineffective, and I'd agree with you, 
but there's no law against the university being stupid, and it certainly
doesn't violate any "contract.")  

As another example, does anyone know if the alt.sex.* and alt.binaries.*
hierarchy is received by Duquesne?  I believe they're a catholic school,
and the restrict those groups (second hand info from someone who used to
work there, but not in CIS.)  Is this censorship or is it their policy?

