I'm going to wimp out on the CMU/sex images/network debate by posting one 
more thing and then running away, but I have to quit posting to let my 
hands heal.  

When we exalt any one legitimate right to a supreme status, hold to it in 
even the most extreme and marginally applicable cases, and refuse to recog-
nize the concerns of our community at large that the right is being abused, 
we do damage to that right and increase the likelihood that it will be compromised.  Examples:

* Even before the recent homicides of abortion providers, a small percentage
  of anti-abortion protesters pushed the envelope of free speech and freedom
  of assembly so hard with their protest tactics that society has pushed
  back with laws that infringe the rights of speech and assembly.  

* Hard core gun owners have insisted that the very real right to keep and
  bear arms must mean that we all should be able to own armor-piercing
  ammunition and fully automatic military weaponry without restrictions.
  They make the classic "slippery slope of rights infringement" claim 
  that even the most moderate and reasonable measures such as registration or 
  waiting periods are bound to lead directly to confiscation and police 
  states.  This position has eroded credibility AND brought on new legislative 
  restrictions.

* Computer users attempt to advance the claim that if institutions do not
  act to supply them, at no charge, network bandwidth and disk space for
  the viewing of illegally reproduced pornographic images for purient 
  entertainment purposes, that institution is infringing the right
  to free exchange of ideas and speech.  The same "slippery slope", 
  no-compromise rhetoric mentioned above is marshalled to summarily dismiss 
  the real concerns of society in this question.

When advocacy of rights takes place in this fashion, and we choose such 
inappropriate or untenable vehicles to make our case, we may end up feeling
warm with the burning convictions of the rightness of our cause . . .
but may lose those rights in our failure to address and acknowledge the
genuine concerns of our opponents.  Glib remarks that "CMU administration
is just hung up about sex" don't demonstrate a depth of understanding of
the issues in question and certainly don't show a desire to even try
to understand why they felt the need to act.

In response to anothers posters comments: I'm familiar with Camille
Paglia's opinions on sexual exploitation and those who argue in a similar 
vein. I can't say much more than that I feel they are mistaken in 
their opinions.

All rhetoric aside, let me exit saying that I do not favor removing 
ANY newsgroups that _discuss_ any ideas or speech, period.  I simply
do not agree that binary image postings are equivalent.  

I'll  read the discussion here and will accept any e-mail 
comments but can respond only to work-related stuff as I try to minimize
my keyboard use in the next couple months.




