From caf-talk Caf Nov 27 18:20:53 1994
From: kadie@eff.org (Carl M. Kadie)
Newsgroups: comp.org.eff.talk,alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk,alt.censorship
Subject: The Chronicle of Higher Education's CMU story
Date: 27 Nov 1994 18:20:52 -0500
Message-ID: <3bb48k$mju@eff.org>

The _Chronicle of Higher Education_ is a weekly newspaper for
academics in the U.S.  It is widely read by university administrators
and is quite influential. The November 16, 1994 issue (page A22-A24)
contained a long article about CMU's newsgroup removal. I believed the
article missed much of the story. Here are my comments on the story. I
plan to take this article and any comments I get about it and turn it
into a letter to the editor.

The Chronicle story's headline was "Carnegie Mellon Eliminated 3
Bulletin Boards With Sexual Themes From Its Computer System". The
headline is incorrect; 3 _sets_ of bulletin boards were removed. The
number of bulletin boards in those sets was 15. Likewise, the
University's original decision was to remove over 80 newsgroups, not 6
as the Chronicle reported. By analogy, if the University removed all
books with titles starting with the letters "A", "B", and "C", the
Chronicle would not report "Carnegie Mellon Eliminates 3 Books from
the Library".

[Aside: Can anyone give me an authoritative count of the number of
bulletin boards that were originally going to be removed? -cmk]

To remove those 80-some bulletin boards would have been to remove
almost all discussion of sex from an important campus medium.

The story incorrectly suggests that "pornography" is illegal in
Pennsylvania. This is not true. What is popularly called "pornography"
is fully protected by the U.S. and Pennsylvania constitutions. The
only exception is for material that is "obscene" for adults or
"obscene" for minors. The legal definition of obscenity is quite
narrow. If material *taken as a whole* as serious artistic, literary,
educational, etc. value, it is protected by the First Amendment and is
not obscene.

The story tells of Carnegie Mellon's legal concerns but it does not
tell that these legal judgments were apparently made without
consulting legal counsel. As Philip Elmer-DeWitt of _Time_ magazine
wrote in the com-priv electronic mailing list:

     [We] found no evidence to support the assertion that CMU acted
     under "the best legal advice." In fact, it's not entirely clear
     that the academic council that voted to ban the alt.sex hierarchy
     had any legal advice at all.

Carnegie Mellon Vice President William Arms wrote the original memo
announcing the ban and was apparently the moving force behind it.
According to Bob Chatelle of the National Writers Union:

    [A]ccording to the minutes [of the student senate meeting],
    [University VP William] Arms made the incredible assertion that
    "while the First Amendment to the Constitution guarantees free
    speech, the state laws of Pennsylvania take precedence."

The Chronicle article mentions that newsgroups ban was triggered by
information about an undergrad research report on the availability of
obscenity. The article was incorrect in stating that this report
focused on Pennsylvania obscenity law. In fact, it focused on the case
law of conservative jurisdictions such as Utah and Tennessee. Also,
according to the Dr. Martin Rimm, principal investigator of the
research:

    [T]he CMU administration never even saw the report. In fact, they
    don't even know the name of the report. [...] They are connecting
    themselves and their actions to our study as an excuse to do what
    they did. [...] What we did was inform [a member of the
    administration] that we discovered imagery on the Usenet which had
    been declared obscene by several courts of law. We gave him the
    names and descriptions of these specific images. We had no
    involvement in any policy decisions.

I fear that readers of the Chronicle story will take Carnegie Mellon's
actions as an example, but the only example Carnegie Mellon actions
provides is a bad one. Readers would do better to rely on competent
legal advisors and to look to their own institution's tradition of
intellectual freedom. Keep in mind, for example, this excerpt the
American Library Assocation's statement on Challenged Materials:

    Freedom of expression is protected by the Constitution of the
    United States, but constitutionally protected expression is often
    separated from unprotected expression only by a dim and uncertain
    line. The Constitution requires a procedure designed to focus
    searchingly on challenged expression before it can be suppressed.
    An adversary hearing is a part of this procedure.

- Carl Kadie
